<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samothrace</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samothrace is a Greek island in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>, dominated by the peak of Mount Fengari. In antiquity it was famed for the Sanctuary of the Great Gods and its mystery cult, where the statue known as the Winged Victory of Samothrace was found. In the 15th century it was held by the Genoese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gattilusio">Gattilusio family</a> before passing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and it became Greek in 1912.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samothrace.jpg" alt="Samothrace" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stpiev · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_Chora_Castle_Samothraki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Samothrace is 17 km long and 178 square kilometres in size. It is one of the most rugged Greek islands, with Mount Saos and its highest peak, Fengari, rising to 1,611 metres. It never became a state of political significance in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, since it lacks natural harbours and most of it is too mountainous for cultivation. The ancient city, whose ruins are called Palaeopolis, stood on the north coast, and considerable remains survive of its walls, built in massive Cyclopean style.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Sanctuary of the Great Gods was the site of important Hellenic and pre-Hellenic religious ceremonies, the Samothracian Mysteries, which like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusinian-mysteries">Eleusinian Mysteries</a> were open to slaves and free people. Visitors initiated there included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> and Lucius Calpurnius Piso Caesoninus, father-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. The traditional account is that the island was first inhabited by Pelasgians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carians">Carians</a> and later by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a>, while the archaeological evidence points to Greek settlement in the 6th century BCE.</p><p>Samothrace came under Persian occupation in 508 BCE, followed by a period of Athenian control, and it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> in the 5th century BCE. It was under Macedonian suzerainty from Philip II until 168 BCE, was then independent, and was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> by Vespasian in 70 CE. In the imperial period pilgrims flocked to the sanctuary from all over the Roman world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> ruled until 1204, when Venetians took their place, and a Genoese family, the Gattilusi, dislodged them in 1355. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> conquered the island in 1457. It came under Greek rule after the Balkan Wars and was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from 1941 to 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The best-known find from the sanctuary is the 2.5-metre marble statue of Nike, now known as the Winged Victory of Samothrace, which dates from about 190 BCE. The French archaeologist Charles Champoiseau found it in pieces on the island in 1863, and it is now displayed, headless, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. It appears on the island's municipal seal, and the Archaeological Museum of Samothrace stands by the sanctuary. The island's population was 2,596 at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and its main industries are fishing and tourism. The port town of Kamariotissa provides ferry links to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samothrace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He surrendered on <b>Samothrace</b>, walked in Paullus' triumph in Rome and died in captivity at Alba Fucens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars at the libraries of Alexandria and Pergamum studied and edited the poems, among them Zenodotus of Ephesus, Aristophanes of Byzantium and Aristarchus of <b>Samothrace</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/odyssey">Odyssey · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The lords of Ainos profited from salt pans, fisheries and river trade with the Thracian interior, and their realm included <b>Samothrace</b> and at times Imbros.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-ainos">Lordship of Ainos</a></li><li><blockquote>Their family extended its power to Ainos, Thasos, Lemnos and <b>Samothrace</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-lesbos">Lordship of Lesbos</a></li></ul></section>
